{"name":"Hardware","hex":"#8b8372","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hardware","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fixtures, equipment, fasteners, tools, and devices used for general-purpose construction and repair of a structure or object. Also such equipment as sold as stock by a store of the same name, e.g. hardware store.","Equipment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hardware"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electronic hardware","blocks":[{"text":"Electronic hardware (HW) consists of interconnected electronic components which perform analog or logic operations on received and locally stored information to produce as output, store resulting new information or provide control for output actuator mechanisms."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_hardware","wikidata":"Q5358364"},{"title":"Hardware (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Hardware is a 1990 science fiction horror film written and directed by Richard Stanley, in his feature directorial debut. It stars Dylan McDermott and Stacey Travis, and also features cameo appearances by musicians Carl McCoy, Iggy Pop and Lemmy. An example of the cyberpunk subgenre, the plot of Hardware follows a self-repairing robot that goes on a rampage in a post-apocalyptic slum."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d0/Hardwareposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardware_(film)","wikidata":"Q1230682"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d0/Hardwareposter.jpg/250px-Hardwareposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d0/Hardwareposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d0/Hardwareposter.jpg/250px-Hardwareposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par